<div class="project">
  <p class="project-title">“Political Violence and the Hierarchy of Exclusion: How Mass Trauma Restructures Outgroup Attitudes.” With Keren LG Snider and Shani Fachter . (<em>Under Review</em>) </p>
  <p class="project-links">
    <a class="toggle-abstract">▶ Abstract</a>
  </p>
  <div class="abstract">
    <p>
How does exposure to political violence shape policy preferences toward different outgroups? We examine this question in the aftermath of the October 7 Hamas attacks. It focuses on the psychological mechanisms through which violent threat translates into exclusionary policies toward outgroups that vary in their perceived association with the conflict. Using an otiginal survey that leverages variation in geographic exposure to violence, we assess how direct and media-mediated exposure operate alongside social identity and threat perceptions. The results reveal that exposure to violence does not independently drive exclusionary preferences. Instead, psychological mechanisms fundamentally structure outgroup hostility. Social identity emerges as the primary driver of generalized exclusion across all outgroups, whereas threat perceptions predict targeted exclusion based on each outgroup's perceived association with the conflict. Media-mediated exposure also operates conditionally, interacting with pre-existing symbolic threat perceptions to amplify exclusionary attitudes. These findings suggest that political violence provides a context for exclusion, but the selection of the targets depends less on exposure itself than on the political and psychological orientations through which violence is interpreted.
</p>
